rel='sections/experiments.tex'
paragraph(rel,'To ensure strict methodological fairness',r'''The archived results use two evaluation implementations. MobilePIXOR and MobilePIXORNeXt use a local loader-aligned BEV evaluator with strict horizontal ROI filtering. PIXOR and the five external 3D detectors use a pinned reference evaluator with native ground-truth handling and preprocessing. Both report 40-point interpolation at the nominal class IoU thresholds, but this does not establish evaluator equivalence. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} therefore reports protocol groups without a shared AP ranking. Accuracy for the seed study was evaluated on an NVIDIA L4; the separate pipeline profiling snapshot for MobilePIXORNeXt and the external detectors used Tesla T4. Native ROI, checkpoint selection, training budgets, and model-stage boundaries also differ.''')
paragraph(rel,'We optimized the model with AdamW',r'''The configured optimizer is AdamW with base learning rate $\eta_0 = 7 \times 10^{-4}$, minimum learning rate $\eta_{\min} = 1.0 \times 10^{-6}$, weight decay $\lambda = 0.001$ on multidimensional weights, and maximum gradient $L_2$ norm $10.0$. Biases, normalization parameters, and loss-balancing logits are exempt from weight decay. Archived run metadata take precedence over a nominal common schedule: the final seed-42 run completed 160 epochs and its AP-selected checkpoint is at epoch 130, whereas final seeds 43 and 44 completed 100 epochs with selected checkpoints at epoch 100. The seed-42 MobilePIXOR run was archived at 38 of 100 planned epochs and evaluated at epoch 30; seeds 43 and 44 completed 100 epochs. The early B1/Binary35 run completed 100 epochs and used a loss-selected epoch-13 checkpoint. Most later historical component runs used a 50-epoch budget with AP-selected checkpoints, often before epoch 50. These records do not constitute a uniform-budget component or random-seed study. T-SBUW balances task losses as defined in Section~\ref{sec:losses_and_oga}.''')
paragraph(rel,'Training used a physical batch',r'''The configured training batch size was 16, with validation batch size 32 and six persistent DataLoader workers. The saved seed-study reports record FP32 inference on NVIDIA L4 with PyTorch 2.11.0 and CUDA 13.0. Offline timing uses batch size 1. The separate T4 profiling report records $98.44\,\text{MiB}$ peak allocated evaluation memory. Architecture, hardware, checkpoint epoch, and evaluation protocol must be matched before using archived timings or AP differences to attribute a component gain. Table~\ref{tab:seed_robustness} summarizes the available runs and explicitly identifies their configuration and budget differences.''')
write('sections/conclusion.tex',r'''We presented MobilePIXORNeXt, a compact proposal-free detector of oriented BEV footprints. Strata-BEV14 summarizes point-cloud densities, elevation statistics, reflectance, and subcell centroid offsets without learned point encoders. A ConvNeXt-style backbone, Block-SimAM attention, scale-gated feature fusion, and grouped heads predict horizontal object geometry. Training combines PC-GTA augmentation, OGA geometric supervision, IQA quality supervision, and bounded multi-task weighting.

The seed-42 checkpoint achieves $71.50\%$ Moderate mAP and $73.45\%$ $\text{mAP}_9$ in the archived L4 local-evaluator report, using $753{,}569$ deployable parameters and 12 additional training-only logits. Its parameter count is $84.41\%$ below PointPillars and $85.85\%$ below SECOND. A separate T4 snapshot reports $71.46\%$ Moderate mAP, $73.42\%$ $\text{mAP}_9$, and $60.96\,\text{ms}$ offline pipeline latency ($16.41\,\text{FPS}$). The historical OGA--IQA factorial study improves Moderate mAP from $67.38\%$ to $70.58\%$ within its earlier focal-context architecture. Historical Q-OGA reaches $69.20\%$ using that earlier architecture without a dedicated quality head. These experiments motivate compact statistical BEV detection, while their differing configurations limit component-level attribution to the final detector.

The evaluation concerns horizontal footprints rather than full 3D cuboids, directed heading, tracking, or closed-loop collision avoidance. Local and reference evaluator outputs have not been shown equivalent. The available seed runs change training budgets and, for MobilePIXOR, input representations; matched repeated runs are needed to quantify initialization sensitivity. Validation uses KITTI's 64-beam LiDAR, leaving sparse sensors, adverse weather, non-planar roads, and embedded deployment untested. Future work will establish a common reference-evaluation protocol, reproduce controlled component ablations, and evaluate hardware-specific quantization on embedded accelerators.
''')

